WEBVTT

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:28.560
Today I am on a journey through border country, land that was fought over for centuries, land where the ancient nations of England and Wales come together.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.440
My travels will take me south through Powys and across the River Severn.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.200
Then it's off to Knighton, along Offa's Dyke then through the Wye Valley to journey's end at Chepstow.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:49.880
Along the way, I'll be looking back at the best of the BBC's rural programmes from this part of the world.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.640
Welcome to Country Tracks.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:02.080
Sometimes you have to climb up high to find out just why the border is, well, where it is.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:16.040
And there you have it, the rolling Shropshire plains there, rising into the jaw-droppingly beautiful Welsh mountain ranges in that direction.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:26.200
This land was fought over for centuries by ancient Britons, Normans and Saxons who all wanted to control it.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.960
And in the 8th Century, the fearsome King Offa built his dyke.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:49.040
Stretching from the North Wales coast to the Severn Estuary, it set a boundary which has lasted more than 1,000 years It's a landscape shaped by centuries of conflict and the spirit of the Borders lives on today thanks to those with a passion for its history.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.640
Just watching these guys brings this whole landscape alive.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.000
Now, Chris, you are a re-enactor, so tell me what's going on over here.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.520
First of all, who are the Welsh, who are the Saxons?

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:14.400
The ones coming up here in the mail shirts are the Saxon patrol coming along the Dyke.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.520
They're on the lookout for Welsh raiders.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.840
Right.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.920
And the Welsh raiders would be coming in to steal livestock?

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.640
Most likely.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:35.840
All it needed was a bad harvest and they'd be going hungry at the end of the winter and they'd be looking to come across the border rather than to steal off their own countrymen.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.320
But the Saxon patrol has spotted them.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.280
And the Welsh aren't going anywhere without a fight.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:04.920
I don't quite understand, I thought that Offa's Dyke was supposed to keep the two sides apart.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.480
Offa's Dyke is more of a boundary.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:14.160
It could be patrolled but it's 175 miles long, you can't defend it all.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:19.160
If I was going to get involved in this, what side do you think I'd be on, the Welsh or the Saxons?

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.840
I think you'd be a Saxon - tall, blonde hair, with a name like Fogle, I don't think that's very Welsh!

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.240
Do you think I could do it in these clothes?

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.480
No.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.120
But I've got some kit we could put on you.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.320
First you're going to have to take that warm layer off.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:47.800
I was liking that with the wind!

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.960
We'll start with the mail shirt.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.840
Do you want to feel the weight.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.000
THEY LAUGH OK, I have to put that on, do I?

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.440
You're now going to wear this, yes.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.440
If you can get your arms right up into the sleeve holes first.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.800
So you really did need someone probably to help dress you, you couldn't do this on your own.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.480
You can get it on on your own when you're used to it.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.000
OK.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:20.399
How many links do you think are on this?

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.800
About 30,000.

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.320
Wow!

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.840
It actually doesn't feel quite so bad when it's on the body, does it?

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.360
The weight's distributed so it's not all in one lump.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:42.480
I'd rather wear it and walk five miles than carry it for one mile.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.720
BEN LAUGHS That sums it up, it's very heavy.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.840
Right, so what next?

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.840
If I put your sword belt on.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.000
If you can actually lift your arms up.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:01.680
Then, when I put this on, this will actually take some of the weight onto your hips so it's not all on your shoulder.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.760
So, how...?

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.480
Arms up.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.280
Arms up higher.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.000
Yes, sir!

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.800
How effective would this be at stopping a sword?

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:15.080
It will stop a cut, but it won't stop a sharp point like an arrow or a spear because that would break a link open and go through.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.640
What else do I need, then?

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.800
Just one?

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.920
Just one because you won't need one on your shield.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.840
And we'll pull the sword hilt round there so it's ready for you to get it if you need it.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.920
That is quite a weight, everything that I've got there.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.760
Wish me luck!

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.640
I'll pass you over to the Saxons now who will give you instructions.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.680
Hello, fellow Saxons!

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.360
Hello, Ben.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:59.560
'And every new recruit has to learn the basics, 'even if I'm stuck at the back!'

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.640
The Welsh that we're dealing with, are they an effective bunch?

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.320
They're very good skirmishers.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:14.160
They are very wily and good at running about which is what we won't do today.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.160
We are going to keep a tightly packed shield wall and we'll drive them back to Wales where they came from.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.040
'But we struggle to push the Welsh raiders back even a few feet 'and it's a standoff.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.680
'Pretty terrifying too, so I'm more than happy to take a back seat.'

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.160
On this journey along the English Welsh border I'm sticking mainly to the Welsh side.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:54.880
But over in Shropshire, Adam Henson took to the skies for a birds eye view of border country.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.880
The Shropshire hills have a distinctive character of their own.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:06.040
Here on the Long Mynd, the sloping hills and valleys make it a haven for those wanting to take to the skies.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:25.000
Mark, why are the Shropshire hills so popular for paragliding and hang gliding?

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:30.680
I think, Long Mynd, this area itself is one of the best in the UK.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.080
We've got all the British records from here.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.200
It's central, it's easy to get to.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:46.600
The views are spectacular and people from not just locally fly here, from all over the UK, world wide it's got a really good reputation.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.880
Are the Shropshire hills really good for the thermals, the uplift?

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:52.240
Absolutely fantastic.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:08:01.000
The thermals obviously in springtime are at their greatest and they can go from a metre a second to 15 or 20 metres a second.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:08.920
Really good lift and they work in the valley very well and the paragliders, because of the way they fly, can turn in them, like birds of prey.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:24.360
It appeals to absolutely everyone, from climbers who are used to that sort of thing right through to people that have maybe disabilities who just want to have a go and experience the thrill of flying and so it has a massive appeal to everyone.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.040
Well, it's got to be time for me to give it a go.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.400
'I've chosen to have a lesson in paragliding.'

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.679
'After unpacking the wing, Mark and I do a spot-check.'

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.919
Make sure that there's nothing untowards in the glider.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.159
'Next for some flight training.'

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.720
OK, so you always fly like this.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.080
OK.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:50.200
If you want to go right, you pull down on the right and push the left up.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:54.720
Keep it to where you want to go and then you release back again.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.120
OK?

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:56.480
When you come in to land, OK?

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:03.360
You then pull both down and then the glider flares, slows down and you can step down nice and easily.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.320
'All sounds simple enough, 'just a small matter of getting airborne.'

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.040
That's it, hands up.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.720
'So, a short run down the slope and up, up and away.'

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:19.040
'Well, perhaps not.'

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:26.080
'But after a few more goes, I start to get the hang of it.'

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:41.880
Well, that was just great.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:49.040
I was off the ground, not far - took off like a beautiful buzzard, landed like a duck!

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.960
The wind conditions have to be right to fly off the top of Long Mynd.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:10:00.800
If it's more than 15 mph, it's not safe for us to fly.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.360
27, wow!

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.560
'It doesn't look I'll get to test out my new found flying skills today.'

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:18.040
Well, against all odds, Mark has told me now that the weather has changed and the wind has died down enough for us to get up in a tandem flight.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.520
I've had my training, now I'm going for the real thing.

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:19.960
OK.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.560
Arm underneath.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:27.280
Both done up OK.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:28.400
Better wish me luck!

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.400
Wow!

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:43.120
This really is a true sensation like a bird flying in the sky.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.840
Amazing!

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:05.240
I'm exploring the border country between England and Wales and my journey takes me near Welshpool and across the River Severn.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.280
There was a time when this river marked the border between England and Wales.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.680
That was, of course before the native Welsh were pushed back into the hills.

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:18.600
So I've come to explore this stretch of water with a craftsman who knows all about life on the river.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:29.560
My guide is Peter Faulkner and he's been making and paddling coracles for more than 20 years This looks a good spot here.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:29.880
OK.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.240
This is where the Offa's Dyke path follows the river.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:37.960
Just over on the South bank, on the right bank, there's the actual Offa's Dyke itself so we're very close.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.440
Perfect.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:40.440
So this is a good place to cross.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.160
This is a good place to cross.

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:46.720
So this is a famous Welsh coracle, made with a cow hide?

00:11:45.880 --> 00:12:00.280
Yes, well, it's a beef steer from the butchers and it's sort of like a big drum really, onto a hazel frame with willow and then it's lashed on with a horse hair cord, the black and white cord you can see, that's plaited horse hair.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.320
It's even got a tail!

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:04.920
It's even got the tail, yes, that's to ward off evil spirits.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:09.880
Now, Peter, I have been in coracles before but not for many years and I was never particularly good at it.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.560
'My first attempt was on Countryfile nearly a decade ago.'

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:25.120
No, no, no.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:26.480
Man's a lunatic!

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.280
Can you give me a quick lesson again on dry land here?

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.240
A dry lesson.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:33.240
Yes.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:37.000
What's going to happen is the paddle will go like a pendulum.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.360
That hand more or less stays there.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:44.080
What you'll do is pull with a slight angle and pull back with a slight angle so it's like a figure of eight, very flattened...

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:44.520
So all I do is...

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.440
You're pulling with that and pushing with that.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:49.320
It's a sort of a...

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:52.800
If I remember correctly, I didn't fall in the last two times.

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:55.760
Yes, I don't like cold water.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:07.200
All right?

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:07.960
That's it, yes, and then the right foot in.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.080
Yes.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.600
And get yourself in the middle of the seat.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.840
Yes.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:12.040
That's good.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.880
There we go.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:15.360
I'll just balance myself.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:15.720
And I'll let you...

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:16.480
You're coming with me, aren't you?

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:17.600
Yes.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.480
Thank goodness for that!

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:23.040
'I'm not that confident yet!'

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.120
So, again, it's that hand up.

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.120
Yes.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.440
That's the right way, isn't it?

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.520
That's it.

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:30.040
Good, you're a brave man.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:30.680
And then you...

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:36.000
Nice and steady, not too quickly.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:36.160
That's good.

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:37.440
Right.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.400
Then if you paddle then out across, as if you were going straight to the other bank.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:42.600
Like this?

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.080
That's good, well done.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:45.640
Yes.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.840
What's the furthest distance you've done in one of these?

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.680
Well, on the Severn actually, 160-odd miles.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.480
How long did that take you?

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:54.440
11 days.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:58.240
I started at Llanidloes and ended up at Gloucester.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:01.080
Do you think anyone's ever tried going across an ocean in these?

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:05.240
I know that a Welshman has been across the Channel.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.120
Yeah, in a coracle.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.680
That is very impressive.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.480
I really don't want to fall in, that's my main thing.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:14.440
Keep paddling toward the bank.

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:14.760
Like this?

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:15.120
Yes.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:17.120
This is fun!

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.600
'Well, it seems coracling is a little like riding a bike.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:27.400
'It all comes flooding back and I must admit 'it's nice to take our time and pootle around on the river.'

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:29.120
OK, thank you, Peter.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:34.280
Now that is the way to cross a river - in a coracle.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:40.600
Now, when actress Jean Marsh came to Offa's Dyke nearly 15 years ago, she did so in a rather more conventional manner.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:15:01.640
Well, I've just climbed to the top of beautiful Burfa Hill.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:02.000
Bit puffed!

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:12.400
Having left my car behind me at the bottom and eventually I will leave behind the beautiful country of Wales as well because this walk spans two different nations.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:18.520
And from the top, I can see just about the whole five miles that I'm going to walk.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.760
A bit intimidating.

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:22.040
It's very beautiful.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:24.840
It also has wonderful historical content.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:30.240
That's why I want to do it and one of the main parts of it is Offa's Dyke, the path.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.400
And I'm now going to set forth and see if I can find it.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:43.320
This circular walk will take me down to Burfa Farm and through the naturalist's paradise at Knill Wood.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:49.120
Crossing the border, I'll find Knill hamlet and then up to the great prize, Offa's Dyke.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.680
An easy stroll then back to Burfa and back to Wales.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:16:01.320
It may sound a bit dramatic, but after all I am an actress, but I don't think I can live without walking.

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:03.520
It's a passion and I love walking everywhere.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:10.360
I was born in London and I'm a Southerner but I feel not English, I feel a part of the whole.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:15.800
Wherever I walk in Britain, I feel as if I belong there.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:22.400
Coming out of the forest from Burfa Hill, now this is our first sign of civilisation.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.200
Old Burfa Farm.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:42.800
Looking at this lovely house now, it is difficult to believe that in 1960 it was so derelict, in such a state of disrepair that it was used as a hen house and there was actually an ash tree growing out of the living room.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:54.160
This is Knill Woods and it's the sort of woods I really love to walk in.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:17:04.440
One of the reasons I like walking on my own, I think best of all, is because animals are aware of you but they're not as frightened.

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:09.200
I don't feel at all solemn about my love of the country.

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:12.400
It isn't a serious thing, it's a joyous thing.

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:16.280
In fact, I mean, utterly inappropriately for my age, I leap about and skip and hop and jump.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.800
Right!

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:34.640
We've now approached passport control.

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:38.640
This is a small step for Jean, but a giant step for mankind.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:42.000
I am about to walk into England.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:22.120
I really like having a walk that has many different points of interest, that's why I like going into Knill.

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:30.280
Cul-de-sac villages are always well protected and Knill has that sort of feudal air about it with all the houses looking rather similar.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:40.240
For me the countryside can be just as holy as any cathedral.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:45.440
An important and integral part of any walk is to visit a country church.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:46.080
Knill has one of the loveliest.

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.720
Is the church always full of such beautiful flowers?

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:55.800
No, we have rather a special occasion at this time of the year.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:19:02.160
We have something called the Open Gardens and we decorate the church just for this occasion.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:14.600
The three main gardens in this hamlet, they're all open for the public to come round, for one weekend.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:20.840
Knill Court was the original house here so I suppose you could call that the original garden.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:36.440
So, Knill Court which was originally the manor house, the centre of Knill, then became a girls' boarding school and it was burnt down in the 40s.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:44.680
Rumour has it that it was from illicit smoking, which reminds me of being a wicked little girl in the film The Belles of St Trinian's.

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:57.200
Of course, this part of the world does have a reputation for a little more rain than most!

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:58.440
But I don't mind.

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:02.720
Who cares about the weather when I'm walking in such beautiful scenery.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:10.200
And none more than the steep climb up Knill Garraway.

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:13.080
Very steep, but worth it for the first glimpse of Offa's Dyke.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:19.960
Offa was the 8th Century King of Mercia who felt it necessary to protect his nation from the Welsh borderland tribes.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:23.680
So, he had this famous demarcation line built between England and Wales.

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:26.120
Well, this is it.

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:29.720
This is Offa's Dyke.

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:32.320
But obviously Offa had a bit of a problem here.

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:34.360
There's a theory as to why it's at such an extreme right angle.

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:40.480
The theory is that in those days there were two separate gangs of labourers hired.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.960
Well, probably serfs in Saxon times.

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:51.880
And they started at separate ends and they met here and had to finish off at this odd extreme angle.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:58.080
You can tell you're on top of a hill, this is Garraway Hill, and the weather is wild and wonderful.

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.320
Right, off we go.

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:13.160
It's so exciting for me to be walking along the very path that so many thousands have before me since, well, the year 750.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:18.560
All those people going about their various businesses and now me, just for pleasure.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:26.240
Well, that's it.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:39.960
Three hours ago, five-and-a-half miles ago I started my walk and since then I've been through every terrain, every kind of weather, I've been through two different countries and I'm about to step back into the other one, way back up there where I first started.

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:46.800
Jean Marsh there enjoying a hop, a skip and a jump around Offa's Dyke.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:47.960
I'm also continuing my border country journey along the Dyke.

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:54.160
I started out in Shropshire and nipped over the border into Powys.

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:58.480
As I travel further along the border, I want to understand more about how and why England and Wales were divided.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.760
And I think Offa's Dyke could hold the key.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:05.360
To unravel some of its mysteries, I'm meeting a man who knows this area like the back of his hand.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:08.440
So what exactly is Offa's Dyke?

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:17.680
Well, Ben, it's basically a big bank and ditch which we're stood on here and essentially it separates England and Wales or what we now call England and Wales.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:31.280
In fact, at the time that it was built, over 1,000 years ago, it would have been separating the Anglo-Saxon kingdom of Mercia, behind you there, and the Welsh kingdom of Powys on this side over here.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:52.240
It relates to a time when the Anglo-Saxons are expanding across Britain and they're basically fighting with the native British people who are represented by the kingdom of Powys and they get to a point where the English king, Offa, of this period, decides it's time to draw a territorial boundary and maybe a partly defensive one and that's really what Offa's Dyke is.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:58.120
It looks to me like it's more of a symbol than an actual physical defence.

00:22:57.640 --> 00:22:59.760
I think that's a good point.

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:01.280
It's very big, it's massive.

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:06.320
Originally it would have been much bigger and a big ditch as well.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:10.960
It would have been a big physical barrier but there were no soldiers patrolling it.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.480
There are no forts, there are no roads.

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:15.360
It's completely different to something like the Roman Hadrian's Wall.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:18.920
It is making a massive statement in that sense.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:30.600
It's just about the power and will of Offa and it's saying, people might be able to cross it, might be able to clamber across it but, you know, you might be in trouble as well if you do that because the guy who's built this thing clearly means business.

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.880
And it must've been a huge physical undertaking.

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.200
Dug by spade?

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:35.560
Yes.

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:38.720
It's people doing this with the kind of tools you'd find in your shed.

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:48.240
I think we're talking of the labour, and it would have been labour, of literally thousands of people to construct this thing quit probably in a short period of time.

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:54.880
You've been studying Offa's Dyke for years, what does it mean to you?

00:23:54.200 --> 00:24:02.520
I think the most evocative thing about it is it's really a symbol of the first emergence of the modern nations.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:14.440
We're right at the beginning really of our modern era and we're just in the time when the languages we speak - be they English as we speak, or Welsh, as people still speak over there - begin to emerge.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:22.880
And here is Offa's Dyke as a very physical symbol of that connecting literally to our cultural origins, to our ideas of being English and Welsh.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:30.200
There's virtually nowhere else in Britain where you can go and physically stand on a remnant of that time.

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:46.200
I've just about reached the halfway point on my journey in the border town of Knighton.

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:50.720
Its Welsh name, Tref-Y-Clawdd, means "town on the dyke".

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:56.160
You can walk the National Trail all the way to Chepstow but, with night falling, I've arranged a lift.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:12.080
Wow!

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:16.000
Wow!

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:19.600
Now, THIS is my kind of taxi.

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.400
Wow!

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:24.680
This is very cool.

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:26.160
Onwards?

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:26.640
Onwards.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:38.440
While I continue my journey in this eye-catching set of wheels, let's go back a few years when I tried my hand at a very different type of four-wheeled transport.

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:46.120
You can't beat the tranquillity of the English countryside.

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:51.280
Yet here in Herefordshire, just over the hill there, there's a whole host of people getting an adrenaline rush.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:11.080
This is now one of the fastest growing extreme sports in the UK with thousands of riders enjoying the freedom of the hills to perfect their jumps.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:13.760
There are now more than 30 centres around the country.

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.360
It is a sport for everybody.

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:16.920
There's an element that everyone can try.

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.720
You can see kids as young as seven picking it up and starting to get on with it.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:23.160
I've seen 55-year-olds riding down the hill.

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:24.800
And you're a course designer.

00:26:23.840 --> 00:26:25.280
Is that right?

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:26.480
Yeah.

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:28.680
Yeah.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:33.960
I do boardercross courses like the ones you can see in the distance, different types of jumps and freestyle setups like the slopes behind us.

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:39.840
Would you say this is a nice, safe environment for these guys?

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:41.040
Yeah.

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:46.800
We've designed this centre the same as all the other centres across the UK so that it is safe.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:49.200
There is somebody at hand to do it and keep an eye on you.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:51.400
It's in the countryside so there's fresh air and beautiful views.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:52.240
What more could you ask for?

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:56.080
How do you go about finding your sites?

00:26:55.240 --> 00:27:05.040
At first, we advertised in a country magazine for farmers with redundant slopes to take a look at our website and check out what the sport was.

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:09.840
We've been going to places like the Earls Court Smithfield Show where 100,000 farmers go through each year over the four days.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:16.800
For farmers to go through and see mountainboards instead of a tractor or a combine was a very eye-catching thing.

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:19.200
We've had results from stuff like that.

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:30.760
For a slope that can only be grazed or mown et cetera, then, for a small amount of money - you're looking at around £5,000 to £6,000 - you can set up a centre and have it successfully running for a season.

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:37.000
It is a cheap way, a fun way, it brings a lot of life into the land and it makes a lot of people happy.

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.400
Right.

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:55.760
Now it's my turn!

00:27:54.800 --> 00:28:02.400
All of you are going to start from this pole here, and we'll head down and try, if you can, to do a powerslide, stopping within the boxes.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:04.920
Once you get to the blue poles, start doing the powerslide.

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:08.200
Once you get to the red poles, try and stop.

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:09.360
Does that make sense?

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:23.680
You're up first.

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:29.000
Brilliant!

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.880
Guys, is this going to hurt when I fall?

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:35.720
Yes.

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:36.040
No.

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.080
As long as I don't land where?

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:38.440
On your bum.

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.160
On my bum?

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:43.720
Keep your weight over the back of the board.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:47.840
Stop yourself falling over by holding the front of the board.

00:28:47.240 --> 00:28:48.400
And then I'm going to push the weight and pull that foot to spin it around, do I?

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:49.360
Yeah.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:49.840
OK?

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:50.040
Er, no.

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:00.320
THEY CHUCKLE Wait.

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:10.480
Weurgh!

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:16.520
EVERY time!

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:33.880
Four years ago, Herefordshire farmer Roy Powell and his daughter Nicki decided to open up a mountainboard track to run alongside their pick-you-own farm.

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:37.520
Now they're attracting people from all over the country.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:41.320
So whose idea was it to set up the mountainboarding?

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:42.000
My dad's.

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:43.520
Basically mine.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:59.840
50 years ago I had terrific fun tobogganing down this hill and then about 20 years ago I looked into having a dry ski slope and unfortunately it was prohibitive cost.

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:04.000
So it more or less rested on its laurels until about 2001.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:09.800
Surfing the net, farmers do surf the net...

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:22.240
..I came across mountainboarding and, luckily at that time, I found Maxtrack which specialised in setting up mountainboarding centres and that gave us, of course, a kick-start to this very venture.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:25.760
And has it breathed new life into your farm, do you think?

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:28.480
Of course, yes.

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:32.680
Any additional enterprise, providing it's run reasonably well, is good and, of course...

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:34.680
It complements everything else we do on the farm.

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:40.320
You get parents coming up to pick fruit, drop their children at the boarding centre so they don't get bored, they're having fun.

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:41.800
The parents do what they want to do.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:42.280
It works well.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:46.000
So, how successful has it been?

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:47.480
It's been very good.

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:50.480
We've been very pleased with the way it's grown from year to year.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:51.800
It's our fourth year this year.

00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:58.360
We're seeing approximately 5,000 per season through the gates, um, and it is growing.

00:30:57.600 --> 00:31:04.280
We're also hosting the national championship final later on this summer, which is really exciting.

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:26.320
Some of these guys behind me are world class riders, including World Champions Leon Robbins and his brother Austin from America.

00:31:25.480 --> 00:31:33.000
The sport is attracting people from the States, Australia and New Zealand - all coming to Britain because it's the best place to do it.

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:38.840
Probably the best place in the world to ride is England.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:49.400
They've got 40 places that you can go ride, slopestyle courses and boardercross tracks and all within three hours of each other so...it's a really concentrated place to come and ride.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:53.560
Would you say that mountainboarding is still in its early stages?

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:54.560
Definitely, yeah.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:57.800
It's been around for ten years.

00:31:57.040 --> 00:32:01.320
It's still just now starting to catch on.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:05.160
You see a lot of kids on the hill and they're into it and their parents are into it.

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:07.480
They have good places to ride now, so it's taken off.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:12.080
Oooh!

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:21.840
Wahoo!

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:32.200
Well, we've had surfing, skateboarding, snowboarding... and now we have mountainboarding!

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:33.560
And, you know what?

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:33.800
This is BRILLIANT!

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:35.280
Woohoo!

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:51.880
I'm back on my road trip and getting to know my chauffeur, Andy Hazell.

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:59.360
He's a border-based artist and self-confessed magpie who collects all sorts of, well, junk and turns it into unusual art.

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:04.680
This old, retired hearse was a perfect canvas.

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:13.960
The funny thing is when I bought this from the undertaker in Anglesey, the other person who wanted to buy it was a goth rock band.

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:14.880
So, it's only me and goth rock bands who want to buy these things.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:15.800
Why cars?

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:17.840
Why do you adapt cars?

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:21.000
Because cars were once sacred things.

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:22.800
Cars were things your dad used to polish on a Sunday.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:25.000
And now, cars are throwaway items.

00:33:24.480 --> 00:33:26.880
You can buy a car for £150.

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:33.000
It's the exact opposite of Pimp My Ride, where people spend a fortune turning it into something sexy and gleaming.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:39.320
This is more about deconstructing the car, being playful with it.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:40.920
Do you call yourself eccentric?

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:43.520
I'm probably seen as eccentric.

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:43.960
I don't think I am eccentric.

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:45.400
I think I'm perfectly normal.

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:47.800
And we're all odd?

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:49.000
Yeah.

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:50.600
And your workshop gives me the impression you're a bit of a Caractacus Potts?

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:54.760
I'm one of life's little magpies.

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:56.160
I do collect an extraordinary amount.

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:59.200
The best thing is, people know I collect things.

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:03.640
One morning, somebody had left me an inflatable boat outside!

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:07.800
RATTLING Was that something...?

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:08.520
Nothing important.

00:34:08.600 --> 00:34:13.040
How would you like to think people received your art?

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:15.400
I say that my job is to put a bit of mystery and wonder back.

00:34:14.960 --> 00:34:19.480
I'd say by the reactions we're getting, you're definitely getting "wonder"!

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:20.679
Confusion.

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:20.960
Yes.

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:22.159
Humour.

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:22.440
Yes.

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:22.719
All of those.

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:26.800
Obviously it's road-legal...

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:27.239
It is, Officer!

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:32.360
I got stopped twice, once by Cheshire police, once by Tyne and Wear, driving this to Newcastle last December.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:37.280
Presumably, they can't believe it is road-legal?

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:37.920
Neither can I!

00:34:37.040 --> 00:34:38.400
Neither can I!

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:39.560
And I'm in it with you.

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:40.760
I'm taking a risk.

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:44.320
Can we get up a hill like this?

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:46.440
Oh, yes!

00:34:45.600 --> 00:34:46.719
Course we can!

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:47.159
I trust you.

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:52.520
You must get some wind drag, though?

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:57.080
There is a fair amount of wind.

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:10.920
Heading through Powys, we come to Presteigne and we're running low on fuel.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:17.680
I'm glad you find that so funny!

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:20.000
Right, on to another petrol station!

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:33.960
The diesel pump is that far away.

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:34.680
The petrol pump is that far away.

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:37.040
The petrol pump doesn't reach by that much, so I'll take the spire off.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:39.880
Lucky you brought the ladder.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:41.800
Have ladders, have spanner.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:44.960
This could be quite a long journey.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:14.680
That's definitely a new one for me.

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:17.840
Have you ever seen a car that's had to remove its spire to get under your roof?

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:18.480
No.

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:22.920
We've had a few horse boxes that haven't fitted well!

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:23.840
But that's definitely a first.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:25.200
What do you think of it?

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:25.720
I think it's really good.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:29.320
I especially like the lights in it.

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:29.760
I'd love to see that at night.

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:30.240
At night, it looks great.

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:31.120
There we go, then.

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:31.680
Have a good day.

00:36:33.320 --> 00:36:34.480
And you too.

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:34.880
Right!

00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:35.240
Onwards!

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:35.760
Onwards!

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:44.320
On the road again, we soon enter the Wye Valley.

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:54.280
My journey started in Shropshire, winding along the border, through Powys, and will eventually bring me to Chepstow and the Severn Estuary.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:02.560
This is certainly a very unique way to explore the borders and I've finally reached the Wye River.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:13.040
Not long ago, John Craven came here for Countryfile and explored the Wye Valley all the way to Chepstow, which is where I'm heading.

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:36.560
On my walk today, I'm going to be straddling the border between England and Wales, because I've come to the Wye Valley, a place of limestone gorges and dense woodland, of historic towns and dramatic ruins.

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:41.920
The Wye Valley makes a remarkable claim - that it's the birthplace of tourism in Britain.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:48.240
The Valley is an internationally protected landscape of great beauty.

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:58.880
It's formed by a 72-mile stretch of the meandering River Wye, from just south of Hereford, to the rocky outcrop of Chepstow Castle, where it meets the River Severn.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:02.000
Down there, through the mist, is Monmouth.

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:06.800
I'm looking at it from a vantage point called the Kymin, 900 feet above sea level.

00:38:05.960 --> 00:38:17.360
Back in 1800, the gentlemen of Monmouth built a monument here, a temple, to the Royal Navy and its victories.

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:22.480
They also built a banqueting house where, once a week, they would meet and dine in great style.

00:38:22.120 --> 00:38:23.480
It became a local landmark.

00:38:22.680 --> 00:38:28.800
Lord Nelson himself was invited here and he remarked on the splendid view.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:30.320
No doubt he had a better day than this one!

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:36.720
One thing's for sure - he didn't use the kind of water transport I'll use for part of my journey down the river.

00:38:36.920 --> 00:38:42.480
Before that, let's pop down to Monmouth and learn a bit more about the historic town.

00:38:56.880 --> 00:39:02.680
It is pouring with rain now in the main street in Monmouth, but that's not deterring these people digging up a garden.

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:04.920
What's going on, Steve?

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:12.600
We had a chance to excavate here and because the archaeology of the street is so outstanding, we couldn't miss the chance.

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:14.960
What sort of things have you found?

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:16.680
That level we're at now is about 1300.

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:27.360
But beneath that, there's something like 5' of medieval floors, dirt floors, full of the remains of hundreds of years of occupation.

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:32.160
What was going on at about the time people are digging now?

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:41.080
This is really a hiatus in the street and in the country, because that level is just after 1300.

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:49.240
They were about to build a large house here and they were overtaken by the terrible floods and the Black Death, which stopped everything in the street for a long time.

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:51.840
What kind of things are you finding as you're digging?

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:52.720
Are you finding bits of pottery and things?

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:59.320
Yes, pottery and occasional coins, but not a lot here.

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:05.000
But we are getting very distinctive pottery of the early 14th century.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:06.440
This is some of the pottery made in Monmouth.

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:07.640
What would this have been from?

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:07.880
A jug.

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:09.960
It's a medieval jug.

00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:15.160
But it's decorated with different colour clays.

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:17.720
And that is distinctive of this period, just before the Black Death.

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:23.280
What about the most famous man ever to be born in Monmouth, then?

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:27.440
That's Henry V, of course, born in Monmouth Castle in 1387.

00:40:27.640 --> 00:40:29.120
You've got Agincourt Square here.

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:32.440
And Glendower Street.

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:35.520
Owen Glendower, who was the great opponent of Henry V.

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:36.880
Does it always rain here?

00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:37.200
It al...

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:38.120
Well!

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:38.960
You are in Wales, don't forget!

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:41.400
So, what can you expect, yes.

00:40:42.600 --> 00:40:45.200
Yes, it's nice soft weather.

00:40:53.680 --> 00:40:56.560
You've been paddling up and down this river for quite a few years now.

00:40:55.600 --> 00:40:56.760
Yeah, quite a few!

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:57.440
Has it changed a lot?

00:40:56.640 --> 00:40:58.320
It's changed...

00:40:58.840 --> 00:41:01.920
The height of it has changed.

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:08.640
It's probably due to the water extracted from pump houses for domestic and industrial use.

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:14.880
This must be the most perfect way of watching wildlife on the river.

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:15.080
It is.

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:18.680
Wildlife...they don't perceive us as a threat.

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:24.640
Having passed through an area, we've left no mark on it.

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:25.480
How clean is it?

00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:32.400
It's a clean river, probably cleaner than it's ever been, I would think, judging by the kingfishers, goosanders and cormorants you see.

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:35.360
There's otters too.

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:37.720
You never see them, but you see their tracks.

00:41:37.480 --> 00:41:39.560
So, it's a nice, clean river to paddle along.

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:39.720
Sure.

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:40.200
Yeah, sure.

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:43.120
Oh, look, there's a kingfisher there, just left-hand side.

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:45.040
Kingfisher where?

00:41:44.080 --> 00:41:45.520
Crossing the river.

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:46.320
I've got him.

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:46.560
Oh, there you go!

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:46.840
There he goes!

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:52.520
Wow!

00:41:51.840 --> 00:41:54.240
It's a while since I saw a kingfisher so close.

00:42:10.880 --> 00:42:14.000
What happened to this bridge here?

00:42:13.320 --> 00:42:26.560
Well, this was a box section bridge and when they were salvaging it, cutting it, the guy this side was stood on it with oxy-acetylene and the foundations gave way and it fell into the river.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:32.520
He was on it, they rushed him to hospital, I guess he was OK cos he came back and finished the work cos it's gone!

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:33.400
He fell in with the bridge?

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:34.880
He fell in with the bridge, yeah.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:35.200
Lucky man.

00:42:34.240 --> 00:42:36.920
It caused quite a stir in the town.

00:42:52.600 --> 00:43:00.520
Well, that's enough paddling, now to the serious business of walking cos there's still about 12 miles to go to Chepstow.

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:07.040
I got off the canoe on the English bank of the Wye and now I'm crossing on this old railway bridge back into Wales again.

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:10.480
Over there, in England, is the village of Redbrook.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:17.840
In days gone by this was an important industrial area - there was lots of tin and iron about - but no trace of that left anymore.

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:33.960
The sun has come out.

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:37.960
Goodness knows for how long, but what a difference it makes to this area, cos it brings out all the different shades of colour, doesn't it, Andrew?

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:39.080
It does, yes.

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:42.040
It's a very special place indeed.

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:52.640
The river here cuts a fantastic limestone gorge, cloaked on either side by semi-natural ancient woodlands, which have existed here since the last ice age.

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:59.880
We have a high density of wildflower meadows, the river itself supports an array of protected species, it really is a very special place.

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:02.880
And how difficult is it to keep it looking special?

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:03.280
It's a challenge for us, certainly.

00:44:02.480 --> 00:44:12.840
We work with all the various organisations, the land owners, the community groups, they're the ones who make a change and influence how the land looks and it's working with them that we achieve that goal.

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:19.400
You must get involved in quite a lot of bureaucracy with England on one side and Wales on the other.

00:44:19.320 --> 00:44:31.800
Yes, it does present an administrative challenge for us, cos we aren't just international, we also go into three government regions and three different counties, so all of the various partners are often duplicated or even triplicated.

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:35.320
What's all this about the Wye Valley being the birthplace of tourism?

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:43.640
It all started with the Reverend William Gilpin, who took a tour down the River Wye in the 1770s.

00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:49.400
From that he wrote a book entitled Observations On The River Wye.

00:44:48.520 --> 00:44:55.280
It was a great success and inspired many people to come to the area, including people such as Wordsworth, Turner and Coleridge.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:10.520
This wonderful viewpoint is called Whitestone.

00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:16.120
When William Wordsworth did his grand river tour of the Wye he climbed all the way up here and wrote these lines.

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:35.200
Though changed, no doubt, from what I was when first I came among these hills, when like a roe I bounded o'er the mountains by the sides Of the deep rivers and the lonely streams Wherever nature led, more like a man Flying from something that he dreads than one Who sought the thing he loved.

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:46.760
These must be some of the most beautiful, most impressive ruins anywhere.

00:45:45.880 --> 00:45:49.120
What's left of Tintern Abbey.

00:45:48.320 --> 00:45:54.280
It was only the second Cistercian abbey to be build in Britain - work started in 1131.

00:45:53.520 --> 00:46:01.000
Eventually there were about 100 of them and, like all the rest, Tintern was knocked about a bit by Henry VIII.

00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:08.720
So who exactly were the Cistercians, then?

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:17.680
Well the Cistercians were Benedictines, a breakaway group who felt that the order wasn't following the rule of Benedict in quite the way it should have been.

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:30.360
So they set themselves up in isolated places like Tintern as an agricultural community - well, they had to, they had to fend for themselves, make their own food.

00:46:29.480 --> 00:46:32.920
They did it very well, didn't they?

00:46:32.600 --> 00:46:34.160
Amazingly well, yes.

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:37.880
They actually constructed the landscape.

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:40.760
In fact, today the landscape is still is often quite Cistercian.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:46.120
Henry VIII's men didn't do a very good job of demolishing this place, did they?

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:46.760
I mean, it's much more intact that most ruined abbeys.

00:46:45.800 --> 00:46:47.320
Indeed.

00:46:47.840 --> 00:47:08.720
I think the reason for that is that the lands went back into the estates of the Earl of Worcester, who was a Catholic lord on the King's Council, and since his chantry chapels were here with his family tombs, he didn't really want to see that all disappear, so he to make some effort to destroy part of the abbey and then he just locked it up.

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:11.680
It was a very remote area.

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:12.160
So the King didn't really know...

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:13.640
No, the King didn't know what was going on.

00:47:12.760 --> 00:47:16.520
So he just took the roof off, really.

00:47:15.560 --> 00:47:16.720
That's indeed what happened, yes.

00:47:25.680 --> 00:47:28.680
Well, here I am at journey's end.

00:47:27.880 --> 00:47:34.080
I started in a border town - Monmouth - and I'm finishing in one - Chepstow.

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:35.480
And over there is it's famous castle.

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:46.120
The castle is Chepstow's pride and joy.

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:50.480
In fact, it's the oldest surviving stone fortification anywhere in the country.

00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:55.840
It was built during the time of William the Conqueror and it still dominates the town.

00:47:55.200 --> 00:48:02.640
The weather on my little trek has been far from perfect, but the buildings and the scenery have been fantastic.

00:48:01.920 --> 00:48:06.680
The Wye Valley is certainly a great place to go walking and canoeing.

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:19.000
Travelling solo once again, I've also arrived in Chepstow, which reveals the most dramatic sight yet of the border between England and Wales.

00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:27.200
I've explored border country from the Shropshire plains to the Welsh hills, Starting near Oswestry, over into Powys and through the Offa's Dyke town of Knighton.

00:48:35.080 --> 00:48:40.000
Then it was on through the Wye Valley and to Chepstow - and a truly awe-inspiring structure, which straddles two nations.

00:48:43.680 --> 00:48:46.600
The Severn Bridge.

00:48:49.040 --> 00:48:54.680
People have been crossing the Severn Estuary since Roman times, but it's a perilous journey.

00:48:53.800 --> 00:49:01.880
The tidal range is the second highest in the world, beaten only by the Bay Of Fundy in Nova Scotia.

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:10.600
There are two bridges over the Severn Estuary, very similar in design and less that four miles apart.

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:19.680
The older one of the two was completed in 1966 as an alternative to the old ferry service, which could only carry 17 cars at a time.

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:23.920
Since the day it opened, the old Severn Bridge has carried over 300 million vehicles.

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:33.160
Oddly, it's two bridges in one, because it spans both the Severn Estuary and the River Wye.

00:49:32.360 --> 00:49:36.360
I took a walk on the Wye side with bridge general manager Jim Clune.

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:40.840
Obviously the main Severn Bridge is behind us.

00:49:39.920 --> 00:49:41.520
We're technically on a different bridge.

00:49:40.760 --> 00:49:42.960
Why have you brought me here?

00:49:42.160 --> 00:49:48.080
Well, the Wye bridge is actually the border between England and Wales.

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:53.200
So, basically that's almost like an island just behind us and this is a different river?

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:54.960
This is the River Wye, yes.

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:58.600
So we're just about to cross into Wales just over here, is that right?

00:49:57.840 --> 00:50:01.720
Yes, as we speak we are crossing the border between England and Wales.

00:50:02.080 --> 00:50:05.800
The thing I notice most is it's very noisy, very windy and very wobbly.

00:50:04.920 --> 00:50:06.240
Is this all normal?

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:07.840
Yes, it is.

00:50:07.120 --> 00:50:15.160
This is a steel structure and steel is a living material, so it is designed to move and it is absolutely safe.

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:18.480
Where we're walking is sort of a public path, is it?

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:18.760
People can walk along here?

00:50:17.920 --> 00:50:22.200
Yes, this is a public right of way for pedestrians and cyclists.

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:24.120
Though not the most pleasant place to come for a walk.

00:50:23.160 --> 00:50:32.480
As somebody who runs this route, I can tell you it can be absolutely invigorating, depending on the wind speed.

00:50:31.520 --> 00:50:33.880
And the wind must really howl through here.

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:36.720
Yes, it does.

00:50:35.840 --> 00:50:46.080
This bridge is occasionally closed during the winter when the wind is very strong, but the design of the deck is such that we can't actually install wind shielding.

00:50:49.640 --> 00:50:55.480
We are incredibly privileged, cos we're in a lift heading up one of the towers of the Severn Bridge.

00:50:54.680 --> 00:51:02.120
Hopefully, shortly I'm about to get the most fantastic view between England and Wales.

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:05.360
This really is a real privilege to come up here.

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:12.080
I've been to the Severn Bridge a few times over the years, but never... up to the top of the tower!

00:51:15.320 --> 00:51:17.800
How incredible is that?

00:51:17.280 --> 00:51:23.080
So, obviously, we've got Wales over there, and England over there.

00:51:29.480 --> 00:51:33.240
Quite extraordinary.

00:51:32.960 --> 00:51:41.080
Travelling the border between England and Wales, I've seen how it's been raided, protected and fiercely fought over.

00:51:41.760 --> 00:51:52.840
Standing here, I see one border which no-one could contest - Britain's longest river as it finally meets the sea, splitting two nations along the way.
